module std.drop;

/// Vyx Standard Library — Drop trait (P4 memory model)
///
/// Marks a type whose values transfer ownership on bind/assign rather
/// than shallow-copy. The compiler emits `self.drop()` at scope exit for
/// every non-moved local whose type implements this trait.
///
/// Contract:
///   - `drop()` MUST be idempotent: second call on the same `self` is a
///     no-op. The canonical way is to null-out the owning pointer and
///     early-return on null at entry.
///   - `drop()` MUST free every owned heap resource (backing buffer,
///     refcount cell, file handle, mutex, ...).
///   - `drop()` MUST NOT call back into user code that could itself
///     allocate — that can re-enter the scope-exit emission and cause
///     cascading drops. Keep `drop()` leaf-level.
///   - `drop()` MUST NOT throw (there is no unwind mechanism in the
///     middle of scope cleanup).
///
/// Move semantics (Sema-enforced, see V-MOVE-001):
///   - `var b = a` transfers ownership; `a` becomes unusable.
///   - `foo(a)` by-value transfers ownership unless `foo` takes `&T`.
///   - `a.clone()` is the explicit escape hatch — returns an independent
///     value so both `a` and the new binding remain usable. For
///     heap-owning containers this is a deep copy; for shared-ownership
///     smart pointers (`Ref<T>` / `Rc<T>`) it is a refcount bump. The
///     two-contract semantics of `.clone()` are documented in full at
///     `std/clone.vyx` (module `std.clone`, lang_item `"clone_trait"`);
///     every `impl Clone for X` in std picks exactly one contract and
///     says so at the method site.
///   - `Rc<T>::clone` is refcount bump, not deep copy (see std.clone).
///
/// Automatic Drop impl:
///   - Any class listed as a heap-owning lang_item (vec, dict, hashmap,
///     hashset, set, stack, deque, list, btree, string, box, ref, scope)
///     is recognised as heap-owning by the compiler and must provide
///     `fn drop()`; the usual form is `fn drop() { self.destroy(); }`.
///   - A user class with a `fn drop()` method in its table is treated
///     the same way (no attribute needed).
///
/// This trait is registered under the lang_item slot "drop_trait" so
/// CodeGen can discover it when emitting scope-exit drop calls without
/// hard-coding the name "Drop".

@[lang_item("drop_trait")]
public trait Drop {
    /// Called at scope exit for non-moved locals. See module doc for
    /// idempotence and no-throw contracts.
    fn drop(self);
}
